# 10. MODULE, SUBMODULE AND FEATURE REGISTRY – CRITICAL FUTURE INTEGRATION

This is a mandatory permanent architecture requirement.

Whenever a new module, submodule, feature, screen or business action is developed in CIPPlatform, it must be registered in the centralized Module & Feature Access Registry.

The registry must not depend on manually remembering to update permissions.

## 10.1 Module Registry

Maintain:

- Module ID.
- Module Code.
- Module Name.
- Parent Module.
- Description.
- Route or Navigation Reference.
- API Namespace.
- Module Owner.
- Module Status.
- Version.

## 10.2 Feature Registry

Maintain:

- Feature ID.
- Feature Code.
- Feature Name.
- Parent Module ID.
- Parent Submodule ID.
- Description.
- Related API.
- Related Service.
- Required Permissions.
- Feature Status.
- Version.

## 10.3 Automatic Permission Registration

Whenever a developer adds:

- A new module.
- A new submodule.
- A new feature.
- A new screen.
- A new API operation.
- A new protected business action.

The development process must register the applicable permission definitions.

Example:

A new module is created:

Supplier Management.

Features:

- Supplier List.
- Create Supplier.
- Edit Supplier.
- Supplier Approval.
- Supplier Bank Details.
- Supplier Deactivation.

The permission registry must identify the corresponding valid actions.

Example permission codes:

SUPPLIER.VIEW
SUPPLIER.CREATE
SUPPLIER.EDIT
SUPPLIER.APPROVE
SUPPLIER.BANK_DETAILS.VIEW
SUPPLIER.BANK_DETAILS.EDIT
SUPPLIER.DEACTIVATE

Permission codes must be stable and unique.

## 10.4 Mandatory Permission Synchronization

Implement a controlled registration and synchronization mechanism.

It must:

1. Identify newly introduced modules and features.
2. Identify newly introduced protected actions.
3. Register missing permission definitions.
4. Detect duplicate permission codes.
5. Detect orphaned permission definitions.
6. Identify removed or deprecated features.
7. Identify APIs without permission enforcement.
8. Identify frontend actions without matching backend permissions.
9. Identify backend protected operations without registry entries.
10. Generate an integration report.

Use explicit module/feature permission metadata or a centralized registration mechanism.

Do not rely solely on scraping frontend menus or automatically guessing permission names.

## 10.5 IMPORTANT – NO AUTOMATIC PERMISSION GRANTING

Automatic permission registration does not mean automatic access granting.

When a new feature is added:

- Register its permission definitions.
- Identify roles that may require the permission.
- Produce a proposed role-impact report.
- Require authorized approval before granting access.
- Default to deny until explicitly assigned.

Existing users must not unexpectedly receive new privileges.

## 10.6 DEVELOPMENT AND CI INTEGRATION

Integrate permission registry validation into the existing development workflow.

Where practical, add automated checks that fail when:

- A protected API lacks permission enforcement.
- A new permission code is duplicated.
- A protected feature is missing from the registry.
- A registered permission references a nonexistent feature.
- A role grants a deprecated permission.
- An unprotected administrative operation is detected.

Every new module or feature must update the Module Register, Feature Register, Permission Registry and relevant governance documentation.

